[ELLIPSIS] 2
  group-span.skool:129: a '...'-spliced continuation — one thought spanning instructions is a '{ }' comment group, not chained fragments
    c$EC5C LD A,(DE)      ; {Copy one font row to the screen...
  group-span.skool:131: a '...'-spliced continuation — one thought spanning instructions is a '{ }' comment group, not chained fragments
    *$EC5E LD (HL),A      ; {...stepping down one pixel row each time.

[GROUP_ENTRY] 1
  group-span.skool:75: a '{ }' comment group is entered at this row from outside its span ($EC2C) — a reader arriving by that jump is handed a sentence about instructions that never ran. Close the group before this row
    *$EC22 LD A,$02       ;

[GROUP_SKIP] 1
  group-span.skool:97: a '{ }' comment group's own branch at $EC3A jumps forward over this row — on that path the rows below the branch do not run, so the sentence covers instructions the reader may never execute. Close the group at the branch
    *$EC40 LD HL,$FD00    ;

[GROUP_SPLIT] 1
  group-span.skool:54: a '{ }' comment group spans 'JR $EC0B' — control leaves the span here, so the rows below it are a second act carrying the first one's sentence. Close the group on this instruction and open the next one below it
    $EC05 JR $EC0B       ;

[LAYOUT_ECHO] 1
  group-span.skool:106: cites a record's field offset ('+0/+1') in prose about code — the layout belongs at the record's own block, stated once; here, name what the field holds
    c$EC47 LD HL,($FC10)  ; Read the slot's +0/+1 position word.

[NUMBER_LIST] 1
  group-span.skool:251: lists numbers ('levels 12, 27 and 33') – a list of the levels, slots or values that hold something restates the game's data, which the data tables show [HOME-3]
    The horned mask ($10) is in the scripts of levels 12, 27 and 33.

[OPERAND_ECHO] 1
  group-span.skool:91: the comment quotes $8A, 138 — a value carried by an instruction inside its own span. Say what the span is for; the row below already shows the byte
